{
  "id": "macedonian_calgija",
  "name": "Čalgija (Macedonian Ottoman-urban ensemble)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Urban ensemble music of Ottoman-era Macedonian towns such as Skopje, Ohrid, Bitola, Veles and Štip, played by professional Christian, Muslim and Roma musicians at weddings, coffeehouses and feasts from the nineteenth century. The čalgija band combined ut (oud), kanon (kanun), violin, clarinet, tarabuka goblet drum and def frame drum, accompanying urban love songs and instrumental dance pieces in makam-derived modes; Macedonian Radio recorded čalgija ensembles in the mid-twentieth century. The toubeleki card stands in for the tarabuka and the dayereh card for the def.",
  "recipe": "classic Eastern European early-1950s čalgija, drinking Song, balkan branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. walnut ribs canonical mid tier oud, kanun tortoiseshell traditional kanun, fiddle, clarinet, toubeleki, dense dance driving dayereh. treated studio. large-diaphragm condenser, German V72 tube pre, Neve transformer console, LA-2A optical, Neve inductor EQ. tape 15ips plate reverb spring reverb.",
